      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-25T20:59:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ack",
      "parentUuid": "32cc1cff_b4947620",
      "revId": "87f4575a29b17c2f61c08a013fda3c41a40e1116",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "34f6e00e_73ad998e",
        "filename": "include/usb_pd_dpm.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-19T16:51:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t totally understand the semantics of the PE sync flag. The PD ready flag puts the DPM in a state where it just checks the value of this flag waiting for something to happen. Then, if the flag is set, it checks the value of other flags that the PE will set. So it seems like the DPM ready state could just check those flags directly and not need this flag. But I see that you remove this flag and function in later CLs, so I\u0027m not too concerned.",
      "revId": "87f4575a29b17c2f61c08a013fda3c41a40e1116",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c6a2c10_726f3082",
        "filename": "include/usb_pd_dpm.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-25T18:11:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This flag is being used at this stage simply for flow control. It\u0027s set at the same point that the previous version of \u0027dpm_run\u0027 would be called. When the message reply state gets added, this flow control mechanism is not required. Without that state, something needs to be in place to prevent DPM_READY from running. Otherwise, prior to the PE finishing its VDM send/response action, the DPM will keep trying to send the same VDM again.",
      "parentUuid": "34f6e00e_73ad998e",
      "revId": "87f4575a29b17c2f61c08a013fda3c41a40e1116",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1abd16b6_b8125c77",
        "filename": "include/usb_pd_dpm.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-25T20:59:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think I can at least frame my question more lucidly now. I see what you\u0027re doing in the message-response-wait-state CL.\n\nIn that CL, it looks like the message wait response state is substantially the same as the idle state. Before the PE is ready, the DPM starts out in the idle state; once the PE has been ready the first time, the DPM toggles between the response-wait and ready states as the PE toggles between the active and ready states.\n\nThe `dpm_set_pe_sync` mechanism in this CL seems like a slightly less refined version of that.\n\nWhy not simply\n1. return to the DPM idle state when the PE exits the ready state (as it will do to send any message requiring a response) and,\n2. signal the DPM that the PE is ready when the PE gets to the point when it would have previously called `dpm_run`?",
      "parentUuid": "1c6a2c10_726f3082",
      "revId": "87f4575a29b17c2f61c08a013fda3c41a40e1116",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "87205eb1_ad90ce9d",
        "filename": "include/usb_pd_dpm.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-01T00:01:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The PE_SYNC flag was only being introduced to replace the message_wait DPM states. But, with that state gone, I agree it makes sense to change where PE_READY is set as you suggested.\n\nThe one potential downside for this is that the calls for setting DPM_FLAG_PE_READY need to remain where they are, so it seems to tie the DPM more closely to the PE in terms of when it runs. But, nothing is being changed in terms of how frequently the DPM state machine will run compared to how often dpm_run() was being called from the PE previously.",
      "parentUuid": "1abd16b6_b8125c77",
      "revId": "87f4575a29b17c2f61c08a013fda3c41a40e1116",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "90e0f29f_6f9d52e1",
        "filename": "include/usb_pd_dpm.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-01T20:45:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the way it is now is OK and good enough to submit and keep working.\n\nSome musings on how it might be improved:\n1. In a later CL, you created another state for Data Reset that is just waiting for the PE to signal that Data Reset is complete. That state ignores the PE-ready flag but uses another flag to do essentially the same thing. For operations where it makes sense, you could do something similar. That\u0027s kind of what you were doing with the message wait state, but I like what you did with Data Reset better, because the transition is triggered by the DPM queuing up a long-running operation, not by the PE doing something the DPM might not even care about. I can imagine doing this for something like VCONN Swap that takes a few PE cycles to finish.\n2. You could move the source of truth for DPM requests inside the DPM and have the DPM states return early if any requests were set, based on the idea that the PE won\u0027t be ready to take any new requests until it has processed all the preceding requests and that the PE will eventually clear all the request flags.\n\nI\u0027m not sure that either of those things would fundamentally improve on the way it works now. Both of them depend on the PE to send a signal to the DPM when it\u0027s \"done\" with something, and the extra states strategy only makes sense if the extra state would be meaningful at the level of the DPM, like Data Reset is.",
      "parentUuid": "87205eb1_ad90ce9d",
      "revId": "87f4575a29b17c2f61c08a013fda3c41a40e1116",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
